WEBVTT

00:00:00.106 --> 00:00:02.206
Scientists can trigger déjà vu.

00:00:02.306 --> 00:00:03.995
Inside a video game.

00:00:04.005 --> 00:00:06.062
It's that eerie feeling that you've lived

00:00:06.072 --> 00:00:07.502
this moment before.

00:00:07.512 --> 00:00:11.052
Psychologist Anne Cleary's
team built virtual rooms

00:00:11.062 --> 00:00:13.677
that look completely
different but share one

00:00:13.687 --> 00:00:14.520
layout.

00:00:14.530 --> 00:00:16.376
When a new room matched one people

00:00:16.386 --> 00:00:20.163
couldn't recall, déjà vu
reports rose, compared

00:00:20.173 --> 00:00:21.821
with brand-new rooms.

00:00:21.831 --> 00:00:23.434
So a memory you can't reach can

00:00:23.444 --> 00:00:24.404
set it off.

00:00:24.414 --> 00:00:25.991
But déjà vu can come with a

00:00:26.001 --> 00:00:27.526
stranger feeling:

00:00:27.539 --> 00:00:29.554
that you know what happens next.

00:00:29.564 --> 00:00:31.366
So the team paused each walk just

00:00:31.376 --> 00:00:33.039
before the final turn.

00:00:33.101 --> 00:00:34.307
Left or right?

00:00:34.317 --> 00:00:36.332
In the grip of déjà vu, people

00:00:36.342 --> 00:00:37.932
felt more sure they knew the next

00:00:37.942 --> 00:00:38.686
turn.

00:00:38.696 --> 00:00:40.746
They did no better than guessing.

00:00:40.771 --> 00:00:42.436
That feeling of knowing can be an

00:00:42.446 --> 00:00:43.621
illusion.
